CORE
alias.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ alias: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ typedef element: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ reference to array of const char: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [basic.type.qualifier]/3 and Note 2: `const T' with T = char[3] (an
alias template's `const T &') and `const A3' with A3 a typedef of char[3]
are arrays of const char.  The front end qualified only the array node, so
`cref<char[3]>' was not `const char (&)[3]' and could not bind to a string
literal ("bad reference initializer", bin53); libstdc++'s
basic_format_string(const _Tp &) was then not viable for std::format's
format string.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only
its WRONG assertion.
